{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790540976,"nonce":"lAVvQCl3JXrt8syzU8XgPl7Birs=","title":"Compiled Wren calls Haxe members through link symbols","message":"In a program built ahead of time, Wren cannot reach Haxe at all. `import \"bench:Bench\" for Bench` in a compiled module is left to WrenLift's runtime import (wlift_aot_resolve_runtime_import), which only finds modules already in the VM, so Bench is null and `Bench.add(s)` raises \"Num does not implement add(_)\". The wasm interop bench's Wren→Haxe column is blank for this reason (a922d85). Haxe→Wren works: Ash links Haxe's caribou natives to WrenLift's #export thunks, with caribou's casts at the boundary (docs/architecture/linking.md).\n\nDesign, the mirror of Haxe→Wren, by linking.md's rule (callee under its link symbol, casts chosen at build time):\n\n- Ash exports a thunk per Haxe member another language uses, named by caribou::link (`caribou_4haxe_5Bench_5Bench_t3add_1`), in WrenLift's convention: one NaN-boxed u64 per argument, receiver first for an instance member, one u64 result. Inside, it casts each argument to the Haxe type with the cast caribou names (the same functions the Haxe→Wren direction uses: caribou_wren_to_float(v, t) and so on, which take the program's hl_type), calls the Haxe function, casts the result back (caribou_wren_from_float), and turns a Haxe exception into the caller's pending error. Only Ash knows the program's hl_types, which is why the casts run on its side.\n- WrenLift makes each imported Haxe class a class of the compiled program whose members call those external symbols, left undefined for the final link to resolve.\n- Caribou decides what crosses: the driver finds each Wren import of a module of another language, describes the Haxe class from the program's bytecode (caribou-ash program.rs already derives TypeRefs for publishing), chooses the casts per member, keeps those members from Haxe's DCE, and hands Ash the export list and WrenLift the foreign classes. A Haxe object that crosses into Wren becomes an instance of that class (the cell and view, as hosted), and back.\n\nMembers with Dyn or Fun types stay on the bridge, as linking.md says.","files":null}]}